    "field": "Ocean-climate interactions",
    "problem": "Climate models exhibit systematic errors in the Southern Ocean, such as warm temperature biases and underestimated sea-ice, because they poorly represent the effects of tide-induced deep ocean mixing on ocean stratification, leading to uncertainty in global climate projections.",
    "method": "To improve Southern Ocean climate simulations, this study compares a standard empirical deep ocean mixing scheme with a more realistic parameterization based on a global map of tide-induced energy dissipation within a global climate model.",
    "task": "The task is to demonstrate, using climate model experiments, how tide-induced deep ocean mixing impacts the climatic mean state of the Southern Ocean, including sea-ice and global ocean circulation, to improve the reliability of climate projections.",
